
Phase 1: Foundation, Columns, & Beams (High-Strength Structural Work)

This phase requires coarse, angular grains with minimum silt to ensure maximum concrete compressive strength.
• Lawrencepur Sand: Sourced from Attock. Coarse and rich in silica. It is the gold standard for high-rise or premium residential gray structures.
• Crush Sand (M-Sand): Mechanically crushed granite/rock. Use this as a controlled alternative if premium river sand is unavailable or too expensive in your area.

Phase 2: Brickwork, Block-Laying, & General Concrete (Masonry Work)

This phase requires medium-grained sand that provides good workability and bonding for mortar joints.
• Chenab Sand: Sourced from Punjab. Medium-grained and versatile. It is the ideal all-purpose option for binding bricks and blocks due to its balanced grain size.
• Ghazi Sand: Sourced from the Indus River. High quartz content with low silt. Excellent for clean masonry and general concrete work in Northern Punjab and KP.

Phase 3: Plastering & Interior Surfaces (Finishing Work)

Finishing requires fine, smooth grains to achieve a seamless wall surface without cracks.
• Ravi Sand: Sourced near Lahore. Fine-grained. Its higher natural fine sediment content makes the plaster mix highly workable and smooth for interior and exterior walls.

Phase 4: Sub-Base, Leveling, & Backfilling (Non-Structural Work)

Strength and cleanliness are secondary here; bulk volume and compaction capability are what matter.
• Pit Sand / Fill Sand: Inland pit deposits. Contains higher clay or soil content. Use this strictly for earth filling, site leveling, and sub-base compaction under flooring.

